WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.871
A student asks how to visit every element of an AVP array.

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:07.419
This example uses a saved run of the real local retriever.

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:10.000
The reply quotes sources; no answer model runs.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.875
The index contains 131 vectors.

00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:14.875
Only 42 passages can enter a student reply.

00:00:14.875 --> 00:00:19.000
Manual AVP chunks keep code blocks intact, with stable source IDs.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Full programs and configuration answers stay outside retrieval.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.710
MiniLM converts the question to 384 numbers.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.742
Source passages use the same encoder and preprocessing recipe for their stored vectors.

00:00:29.742 --> 00:00:34.000
These unit vectors describe similarity; their components do not name concepts.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
These three selected vectors span at most three dimensions.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.111
An orthonormal change of coordinates preserves their lengths and pairwise angles.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:50.667
The camera moves around that real three-dimensional span, then stops.

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:54.000
Search still uses all 384 dimensions.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.933
For each passage, search multiplies matching vector components and adds all 384 products.

00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:04.667
That gives cosine similarity for unit vectors.

00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:10.000
The real ranking places the for-loop passage at position 2.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.500
Retrieval returns the original text attached to the vector.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:22.000
The quoted AVP loop visits zero through length minus one and closes with end for.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The interpreter checked this snippet; that does not review all teaching prose.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.100
The old frontend keeps one tutor request with the question and student context, and receives one text answer.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.600
The backend sends only the question to this RAG lookup.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.000
Code blocks display as examples; they do not change the student's editor.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.556
Ask for while-loop syntax.

00:01:47.556 --> 00:01:52.222
The current corpus lacks that passage, but similarity still returns for-loop text.

00:01:52.222 --> 00:01:54.167
This is a recorded limitation.

00:01:54.167 --> 00:02:00.000
We need coverage checks and answer relevance evaluation before treating every retrieval as useful help.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.811
The new challenge set found expected sources for 15 of 15 supported questions, but only 12 entered replies.

00:02:06.811 --> 00:02:09.838
5 of 9 unsupported questions received no quote.

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:14.000
Keep these failures, review new sources, and test live generation separately.
